VERDICT: REJECTED

The ImgBB upload flow is functionally wired correctly, but the new `/api/imgbb-upload` endpoint exposes the server’s ImgBB credential as an unrestricted public upload proxy. Any unauthenticated caller can POST arbitrary base64 payloads (up to 8 MB per request) directly to this route, consume the account’s quota, and use the application to host abusive content. This is a concrete access-control and API-key abuse risk, not merely a hardening concern.

The client correctly replaces local data URLs with returned ImgBB URLs, handles replacement uploads, and the existing test suite plus production build pass. No secret value is exposed in the diff.

Security: serious broken access control on a credential-backed external-service endpoint.

Next actions:
1. Require an authenticated, server-verifiable user/session authorization check before forwarding uploads (and return 401/403 otherwise); do not rely on the browser UI or CORS.
2. Add server-side abuse controls appropriate to the deployment (per-user/IP rate and concurrent-upload limits, request timeout); retain the current size/type checks.
3. Add route-level tests covering missing authorization, invalid/oversize inputs, provider failure, and successful URL forwarding.

